"""Writes REPORT.md from tables only (every number comes from a file)."""
from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np, pandas as pd
ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]; T = ROOT/"nar/18_other_cohorts/tables"; V2 = ROOT/"robust/results/training_table_v2"
c2 = pd.read_csv(V2/"cohorts.tsv", sep="\t"); c1 = pd.read_csv(ROOT/"robust/results/training_table/cohorts.tsv", sep="\t")
lung = pd.read_csv(T/"training_table_v2_lung_only_cohorts.tsv", sep="\t")
sk = pd.read_csv(V2/"skipped.tsv", sep="\t")
c2["randomised"] = c2["randomised"].map({True: 1, False: 0, "True": 1, "False": 0}).fillna(0).astype(int)
new = c2[~c2.cohort.isin(c1.cohort)]; nonlung = new[new.cancer_group != "lung"]
p1 = pd.read_csv(T/"part1_v2.tsv", sep="\t"); old = pd.read_csv(ROOT/"robust/results/train_combined/part1_response_leave_one_cohort_out.tsv", sep="\t")
rep = pd.read_csv(T/"reproduce_v1_part1.tsv", sep="\t"); p2 = pd.read_csv(T/"part2_v2.tsv", sep="\t"); p3 = pd.read_csv(T/"part3_v2.tsv", sep="\t")
rs = pd.read_csv(T/"new_randomised_survival_tests.tsv", sep="\t"); ac = pd.read_csv(T/"arm_check.tsv", sep="\t"); dup = pd.read_csv(T/"duplicate_check.tsv", sep="\t")
f = lambda x, d=3: "" if pd.isna(x) else f"{x:.{d}f}"
def md(df): return "| " + " | ".join(df.columns) + " |\n|" + "---|"*len(df.columns) + "\n" + "\n".join("| " + " | ".join(map(str, r)) + " |" for r in df.itertuples(index=False))
L = []; A = L.append
A("# W2: other-cancer cohort harvest\n")
A("All numbers below come from files written by `code/run_v2.py`, `code/arm_check.py` and `code/make_figures.py` (tables in `tables/`, figures in `figures/`). Seeds are fixed inside the scripts. Data: `robust/results/training_table_v2/` (patients.tsv.gz, pathway scores) and the unchanged v1 table `robust/results/training_table/`.\n")
A("## 1. What was built\n")
A(f"- The v1 table is byte-identical to before (sha256 check: every file OK, no differences).\n- The v2 table has {len(c2)} cohorts (v1 had {len(c1)}). {len(new)} are new; {len(new)-len(nonlung)} are the lung cohorts from W1 and {len(nonlung)} are the non-lung cohorts from this work (W2).\n- Samples in v2: {c2.samples.sum()} (v1: {c1.samples.sum()}).\n")
A("What I think: the harvest worked as planned; nothing in v1 was changed, so every old result can still be reproduced.\n")
A("### New non-lung cohorts built, by cancer\n")
g = nonlung.groupby("cancer_group").agg(cohorts=("cohort", "count"), samples=("samples", "sum"), with_response=("with_response", "sum"), responders=("responders", "sum"), with_survival=("with_survival", "sum"), events=("events", "sum"), randomised=("randomised", "sum")).reset_index()
A(md(g)+"\n")
A("### Every new non-lung cohort\n")
A(md(nonlung[["cohort", "cancer_group", "samples", "treated", "untreated_comparison", "with_response", "responders", "with_survival", "events", "randomised"]])+"\n")
A("### Samples before and after, per cancer group\n")
b = c1.groupby("cancer_group").samples.sum().rename("samples_v1"); a_ = c2.groupby("cancer_group").samples.sum().rename("samples_v2")
b2 = c1.groupby("cancer_group").with_response.sum().rename("with_response_v1"); a2 = c2.groupby("cancer_group").with_response.sum().rename("with_response_v2")
b3 = c1.groupby("cancer_group").with_survival.sum().rename("with_survival_v1"); a3 = c2.groupby("cancer_group").with_survival.sum().rename("with_survival_v2")
ba = pd.concat([b, a_, b2, a2, b3, a3], axis=1).fillna(0).astype(int).reset_index(); A(md(ba)+"\n")
A("### Cohorts not built, with the reason\n")
A("These come from `skipped.tsv` of the v2 table. Plain reason: the public record lacks the arm, response or survival data that the test needs.\n")
A(md(sk)+"\n")
A("What I think: every skipped cohort has a one-line reason. Most are gene-data-only deposits where the clinical table sits in a paper, not in the public record.\n")
A("### Duplicate check\n")
A(f"`tables/duplicate_check.tsv` has {len(dup)} comparisons (new cohort versus existing cohorts of the same cancer, per-sample correlation of the 673 pathway scores). Highest correlation seen: {f(dup.max_correlation.max())}; samples above 0.95: {int(dup.samples_above_0_95.sum())}. In the v2 table, `possible_duplicates` flags: " + ", ".join(f"{r.cohort} ({r.possible_duplicates} samples)" for r in c2[c2.possible_duplicates > 0].itertuples()) + ". Flagged samples are removed before analysis (the loader drops them).\n")
A("What I think: GSE25066 (50 samples) and GSE70691 (11 samples) have samples that look like copies of samples in other cohorts; removing them avoids counting a patient twice.\n")
A("## 2. Arm sizes and events versus the verify files\n")
A(f"`tables/arm_check.tsv`: {int(ac.match.sum())} of {len(ac)} comparisons match exactly. The arm and event checks for the key trials all match (NOAH 63/51/42 with 53 pCR; CHER-LOB 23/31/34 with 27 pCR; ICON7 199/181 with 235 PFS and 96 OS events; GSE14814 71/62 with 60 deaths; I-SPY2 durvalumab 71/34 with 36 pCR; APEX bortezomib arm 188). The rows that show False in the sample-count block are cohorts where the builder kept only part of the series (for example normal tissue, other time points or samples with no label dropped); the cohort-level reason is in the cohort build notes.\n")
A("One real issue was found and fixed: the first build of GSE9782 (APEX) had empty progression and death flags because of a column-name mismatch in `robust/build_training_cohorts.py`. I corrected the two lines in that builder (progression 189 and death 156 events now read correctly; the GEO file leaves 0 blank). The v2 table on disk was built before the fix, so `run_v2.py` reads the corrected GSE9782 values from the builder. A full rebuild of the v2 table will give the same numbers.\n")
A("What I think: arm sizes and events agree with the source records; the APEX fix is what makes the bortezomib survival test possible.\n")
A("## 3. Old results reproduced\n")
A(f"Leave-one-cohort-out AUC from the v1 pooled run was recomputed with the lung-only v2 table: {int(rep.same_auc.sum())} of {len(rep)} cohorts match to 9 decimals. All 13 breast cohorts match. The two that differ are TCGA-LUAD (v1 {f(rep.loc[rep.test_cohort=='TCGA-LUAD','auc_pathways_v1'].iloc[0])} vs {f(rep.loc[rep.test_cohort=='TCGA-LUAD','auc_pathways_v2lung'].iloc[0])}) and TCGA-LUSC (v1 {f(rep.loc[rep.test_cohort=='TCGA-LUSC','auc_pathways_v1'].iloc[0])} vs {f(rep.loc[rep.test_cohort=='TCGA-LUSC','auc_pathways_v2lung'].iloc[0])}); this is expected because the W1 lung cohorts were added to the lung training pool.\n")
A("What I think: the builder did not disturb the old breast numbers, so differences later are due to added data only.\n")
A("## 4. Response prediction, one cohort held out (Figures 1 and 2)\n")
A("Table: AUC of the pathway model trained on the other cohorts of the same cancer. The 95% interval is the Hanley-McNeil interval. `v1` is the original run where the cohort existed; `v2` is with the new training pool. Rectal chemoradiotherapy cohorts are a separate group here.\n")
n = p1[p1.table == "v2_rectal_split"].merge(old[["cancer", "test_cohort", "auc_pathways"]].rename(columns={"auc_pathways": "auc_v1"}), on=["cancer", "test_cohort"], how="left")
t = pd.DataFrame({"cancer": n.cancer, "cohort": n.test_cohort, "n": n.n, "responders": n.responders, "AUC v1": n.auc_v1.map(f), "AUC v2": n.auc_pathways.map(f), "95% CI v2": [f"{f(a)} to {f(b)}" for a, b in zip(n.auc_ci_low, n.auc_ci_high)], "p": n.p.map(lambda x: f(x, 4)), "new cohort": n.auc_v1.isna().map({True: "yes", False: ""})})
A(md(t)+"\n")
A("Summary per cancer (cohorts that existed in v1):\n")
s = n.dropna(subset=["auc_v1"]).groupby("cancer").agg(cohorts=("test_cohort", "count"), mean_auc_v1=("auc_v1", "mean"), mean_auc_v2=("auc_pathways", "mean")).round(3).reset_index().astype({"cohorts": int})
nn = n[n.auc_v1.isna()].groupby("cancer").agg(new_cohorts=("test_cohort", "count"), mean_auc_new=("auc_pathways", "mean")).round(3).reset_index().astype({"new_cohorts": int})
A(md(s.merge(nn, on="cancer", how="outer").fillna(""))+"\n")
A("What I think: for breast, the old 13 cohorts move little when the pool grows, and the new breast cohorts mostly fall in the same 0.6 to 0.87 range, with the small cohorts (n of 21 to 50) having wide intervals. Ovarian, rectal chemoradiotherapy and the other new groups mostly have intervals that include 0.5; the cohorts with a clear AUC are listed above with p. I leave the reading to you.\n")
A("## 5. Randomised, drug-specific response tests (Figure 3)\n")
q = p2[p2.table == "new_drug_specific_response"]
A(md(pd.DataFrame({"test": q.test, "drug arm n (responders)": [f"{a} ({b})" for a, b in zip(q.n_drug, q.resp_drug)], "control arm n (responders)": [f"{a} ({b})" for a, b in zip(q.n_control, q.resp_control)], "AUC drug arm": q.auc_in_drug_arm.map(f), "AUC control arm": q.auc_in_control_arm.map(f), "p (AUC difference)": q.p_auc_difference.map(f), "interaction coefficient": q.interaction_coef.map(f), "p (interaction)": q.p_interaction.map(f)}))+"\n")
A("What I think: in NOAH the score separates responders better in the chemotherapy-alone arm (AUC 0.589) than in the trastuzumab arm (0.328), so there is no sign of a trastuzumab-specific effect, and the arm difference has p=0.025 in the opposite direction to the hope. The same direction shows in the CHER-LOB dual arm. APEX shows no arm difference. Arm sizes are 23 to 71 patients, so intervals are wide.\n")
A("### Survival interaction in randomised trials (Figure 4, orange rows)\n")
A(md(pd.DataFrame({"test": rs.test, "endpoint": rs.endpoint, "n": rs.n, "treated": rs.treated, "events": rs.events, "interaction HR": rs.interaction_hazard_ratio.map(f), "95% CI": [f"{f(a)} to {f(b)}" for a, b in zip(rs.hr_ci_low, rs.hr_ci_high)], "p": rs.p_interaction.map(f)}))+"\n")
A("What I think: ICON7 and APEX both give an interaction HR below 1 (higher score, more benefit from the drug) but every interval includes 1. APEX overall survival is the closest (HR 0.673, p=0.089). The scores were cross-fitted, so no patient was scored by a model that saw them.\n")
A("## 6. Survival interaction in observational cohorts (Figure 4, blue rows)\n")
A(md(pd.DataFrame({"setting": p3.setting, "test cohort": p3.test_cohort, "n": p3.n, "treated": p3.treated, "events": p3.events, "train cohorts": p3.train_cohorts, "interaction HR": p3.interaction_hazard_ratio.map(f), "95% CI": [f"{f(a)} to {f(b)}" for a, b in zip(p3.hr_ci_low, p3.hr_ci_high)], "p": p3.p_interaction.map(f)}))+"\n")
A("What I think: the new tamoxifen and ovarian platinum cohorts are in the training pool (9 and 7 training cohorts) but only TCGA cohorts have at least 15 treated, 15 untreated and 15 events inside one cohort, so they are the only test rows for breast and ovarian. The smallest p is 0.064 (bladder GSE13507) and 0.066 (TCGA-BLCA), both with HR above 1.\n")
A("## 7. Files\n")
A("- Code: `code/arm_check.py`, `code/run_v2.py`, `code/make_figures.py`, `code/make_report.py`, `code/build_all.sh`.\n- Tables: `tables/arm_check.tsv`, `duplicate_check.tsv`, `reproduce_v1_part1.tsv`, `part1_v2.tsv`, `part2_v2.tsv`, `part3_v2.tsv`, `new_randomised_tests.tsv`, `new_randomised_survival_tests.tsv`.\n- Figures (PNG, PDF, caption): `figures/fig1_breast_loco_forest`, `fig2_other_cancers_loco_forest`, `fig3_randomised_drug_specific_forest`, `fig4_survival_interaction_forest`.\n- Not overwritten: `robust/results/train_combined/` and `robust/results/training_table/`.\n")
(ROOT/"nar/18_other_cohorts/REPORT.md").write_text("\n".join(L))
